cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Zero Knowledge on the Bench: twenty-four embedded projects that prove without revealing"
message: "If you refer to this work, please cite it as below."
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Pagaran
    given-names: Joseph Ambrose
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-7595-9070"
abstract: >-
  Twenty-four embedded hardware and firmware projects that take one paper on
  zero-knowledge model checking apart into the primitives it is built from and
  put each one on hardware already present on a single bench: three Raspberry
  Pis, a NanoPi NEO Air, an STM32H7A3 development board, an ESP32 and an
  ESP8266, three cellular modules, a CAN and RS485 expansion board, a data
  acquisition board, two sensor shields, an industrial sensor node, a
  time-of-flight sensor, a display and four indicator modules.

  Each chapter names a secret, a public statement and the relation between them
  in set notation, proves the property of the primitive it uses with every step
  written out, gives the protocol as a sequence, shows the code in the language
  that chapter chose, and prices the work on the board with an instrument that
  is named. Fourteen languages appear across the chapters. The primitives
  covered include Pedersen and KZG commitments, batch vanishing proofs, Farkas
  witnesses and linear programming duality, Bulletproofs range proofs, one-shot
  folding under a progression-free exponent schedule, sigma protocols,
  Groth16, PLONK and sumcheck-based circuits, recursive composition, and
  zero-knowledge virtual machines.

  Nothing in the volume has been run on the bench it describes. Every timing and
  every agreement rate reads as not measured and names the instrument that would
  fill it; the only measured figures are the source paper's own and are
  attributed to the paper's own machine. A figure the volume derives is marked
  as computed and shows its arithmetic. Where a language model appears it
  proposes and never decides, and the chapter carries the theorem that says a
  wrong proposal cannot cost soundness.
keywords:
  - zero-knowledge proofs
  - model checking
  - formal verification
  - embedded systems
  - polynomial commitments
  - Farkas lemma
  - folding schemes
  - zero-knowledge virtual machines
  - linear temporal logic
  - Buechi automata
  - Raspberry Pi
  - STM32
  - controller area network
  - technical writing
license: MIT
repository-code: "https://github.com/ambrosiobing/embedded-zkmc"
version: "1.0.0"
date-released: "2026-11-06"
